    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the case *Sterling v. Maritime Logistics Ltd*, [2023] EWCA Civ 402, the Court established a nuanced jurisprudential dictum on the duty of care, specifically delineating the applicability of 'force majeure' clauses in maritime logistics contracts under the predictive umbrella of the Merchant Shipping Act 1995. Grounding its judgment in the liability principle demarcated in *Donoghue v Stevenson* [1932] AC 562, the Court held that a logistics provider's duty of care does not become nullified in the face of predictable adverse weather conditions but must instead be balanced against the foreseeability of such events. This interpretation requires contract parties to predetermine the ramifications of foreseeable meteorological conditions, directing an analytical framework that insists upon a nuanced weighing of contractual clauses' applicability—a contrast to a strictly pragmatic interpretation seen in the prior case *Maritime Holdings v Global Trade* [2015] EWHC 124 (Comm). The Court's holding, therefore, underscores a heightened judicial scrutiny applicable to 'force majeure' clauses, with contractors expected to prove infeasibility of performance rather than mere aggravation of difficulty, thereby ensuring the safety and predictability of the maritime logistics sector. \nThe rationale aligns with the legal",
      "source": "target",
